          "question": {
            "text": "$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}$ gas when passed through a solution of cations containing $\\mathrm{HCl}$ precipitates the cations of second group of qualitative analysis but not those belonging to the fourth group. It is because",
            "image": null
          },
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "chNcX",
              "text": "presence of HC1 decreases the sulphide ion concentration.",
              "image": null,
              "isCorrect": true
            },
            {
              "id": "K3qST",
              "text": "solubility product of group II sulphides is more than that of group IV sulphides.",
              "image": null,
              "isCorrect": false
            },
            {
              "id": "3nsU6",
              "text": "presence of HCl increases the sulphide ion concentration.",
              "image": null,
              "isCorrect": false
            },
            {
              "id": "GCLzL",
              "text": "sulphides of group IV cations are unstable in $\\mathrm{HC} 1$",
              "image": null,
              "isCorrect": false
            }
          ],
          "correctValue": null,
          "solution": {
            "text": "IV $^{\\text {th }}$ group needs higher $\\mathrm{S}^{2-}$ ion concentration. In presence of $\\mathrm{HCl}$, the dissociation of $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}$ decreases hence produces less amount of sulphide ions due to common ion effect, thus $\\mathrm{HCl}$ decreases the solubility of $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}$ which is sufficient to precipitate II $^{\\text {nd }}$ group radicals.",
            "image": null
          },
